{
  "dom_id": "step-q323087",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226602/assets/ec1381a50c493fce0f5017e706251c68c1a8a20ba5b68ef268d5905aa20772e6.png)\n\nThe circles $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘})$ in degrees.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$",
  "worked_solution": "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={45}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={135}^{∘}.$ \n\nAlso, notice that $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(4\\sqrt{2},{135}^{∘}).$",
